                    briefing_text_r2:       `The cure notice forces a posture. A pure <em>visionary reframe</em> asks the customer for time and misses the 90-day unit; a pure <em>managerial sprint</em> hits the unit but leaves the board with no direction. Laljani's finding bites here: strategic leadership is <strong>both</strong>. Choose your posture, then allocate the <strong>£3.5M recovery budget</strong> across the missing power-electronics expertise, the test rig and engineering hands — the full plan costs £6.2M, so you must choose. Finally, decide what you commit to the board and the customer, and make the two stories cohere.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — The stretch assignment`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Leaders are made in the deep end. You must assign leadership of the qualification sprint — and you can develop a leader <em>while</em> saving the contract. Choose the <strong>proven deputy</strong> (safe, but it stretches no one) or the <strong>high-potential engineer</strong> (visionary, unproven). Then design the stretch so it builds rather than drowns: real authority with guardrails, a support scaffold of mentoring, escalation and day-job cover, and a halfway checkpoint with a fallback. Throw the high-potential in with no scaffold and you are not stretching them — you are drowning them.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Deliver, develop and win confidence`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Day 60 of 90. One subsystem is behind, a flight-risk engineer has resigned, and the board wants a progress read. <strong>Recover the slipping subsystem</strong> within budget, <strong>coach the stretch leader</strong> through the setback — coaching builds a leader, rescuing un-builds one — make a <strong>retention move</strong> on the two remaining flight risks (£0.9M exposure), and present a 3-minute board read that links the 90-day delivery to the long-term transition. The trap: over-claiming to buy peace, and grabbing the wheel the moment your stretch leader wobbles.`,

                    r1_root_label:          `Why has the drivetrain programme missed its launch twice?`,
                    r1_intent_label:        `Draft the one-line strategic intent for the division`,
                    r1_mustwin_label:       `Name the single 90-day must-win`,
                    r1_stoplist_label:      `Stop-list discipline: how decisively do you stop low-priority work to free capacity for the must-win? (%)`,
