                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>Right diagnosis.</strong> It is a capability gap AND a focus gap — no senior power-electronics lead and effort spread thin across too many priorities. Two failed launches are rarely one cause. Now you can fix what is actually broken instead of treating a symptom.`,
                    coach_r1_capability:    `<strong>Half the picture.</strong> Yes, you are missing a senior power-electronics lead — but one hire will not fix a programme that has lost focus across too many priorities. Capability AND focus failed here, not capability alone.`,
                    coach_r1_execution:     `<strong>Careful — “they worked too slowly” is the comfortable answer.</strong> A team that misses twice usually has a capability or focus problem, not a pace problem. Diagnose the real gap or you will sprint hard at the wrong target.`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>Blaming the last MD and “weak engineers” is the easy story.</strong> Replace people and you still have the capability gap and the unclear focus that sank both launches. This is a system and direction problem, not a people-purge.`,
                    coach_r1_direction_sharp: `<strong>That is a strategic leader's opening.</strong> You named the real gap AND set a clear, deliverable direction — “the UK leader in clean motion”. That is an intent the board can back and a team can rally to, not a slogan and not a budget line.`,
                    coach_r1_grand:         `<strong>Inspiring — but is it a direction or a slogan?</strong> “Decarbonise the planet” will not tell an engineer what to build by Friday. Strategic intent is ambitious AND specific to what this division is for. Sharpen it to clean motion the customer can trust.`,
                    coach_r1_budget:        `<strong>That is a target, not a direction.</strong> “Hit the numbers and protect the margin” is administration. The board appointed you to say where the division is going. A financial goal is the result of a strategy, not the strategy.`,
                    coach_r1_nointent:      `<strong>No intent is the classic trap.</strong> You may win the contract and still lose the mandate when the board asks “where is this division going?” and your answer is a Gantt chart. They appointed an MD to set direction — set it now, not later.`,
                    coach_r1_everything:    `<strong>“Everything at once” is no must-win at all.</strong> With two failed launches and a 90-day clock, spreading thin is exactly what sank you before. Pick the one win — the qualified unit — and stop-list the rest.`,
                    coach_r1_morale:        `<strong>Morale matters, but it follows a win.</strong> A demoralised team is re-energised by delivering the unit, not by a morale programme that delays it. Make the must-win the unit; let the early delivery rebuild belief.`,
                    coach_r1_nostop:        `<strong>No stop-list, no capacity.</strong> You cannot add a 90-day sprint to a team already spread across too many priorities and expect it to land. Saying no to low-priority work is how you free the hands to deliver the must-win.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That is strategic leadership.</strong> A disciplined sprint to hit the 90-day unit, framed as step one of the clean-motion transition — you are managing AND leading. The customer gets delivery; the board gets direction; the two stories reinforce each other.`,
                    coach_r2_capgap_funded: `<strong>Both right — posture and money.</strong> A balanced sprint AND real funding into the missing power-electronics expertise. You are not just choosing the right posture, you are resourcing the actual capability gap that sank the last two launches.`,
                    coach_r2_visiononly:    `<strong>Beautiful reframe — and the contract is cancelled.</strong> The cure notice expires in 90 days; asking for time to co-develop a roadmap means no qualified unit and no contract. Laljani's point exactly: visionary without managerial is a speech. What is your vision worth once the £34M walks?`,
                    coach_r2_mgmtonly:      `<strong>You will probably hit the unit — and still lose the mandate.</strong> When the board asks where the division is going, “here is the sprint plan” is a project manager's answer, not an MD's. You have managed, not led. Where is the direction?`,
                    coach_r2_capgap_starved: `<strong>You starved the actual gap.</strong> The thing that sank both launches is the missing senior power-electronics expertise — and you funded almost everything except that. Money on overtime will not buy the capability you do not have.`,
                    coach_r2_overpromise:   `<strong>You promised the board a full strategy AND the customer an early unit AND no overrun.</strong> You can deliver at most two. When the third breaks, you have spent the one asset a new MD cannot refund — credibility. Commit to what is bold AND true.`,
                    coach_r2_split:         `<strong>Two stories that do not cohere will collide.</strong> The board and the customer talk. Tell them different things and the day they compare notes, your credibility is gone — and credibility is the only thing buying you the time strategy needs.`,
                    coach_r3_stretch_scaffold: `<strong>This is how leaders are made.</strong> You stretched the high-potential AND built the scaffold — mentoring, escalation, cover — that makes a stretch survivable. They lead the hardest sprint in the firm's history with a net under them. That develops a leader rather than drowning one.`,
                    coach_r3_stretch_noscaffold: `<strong>That is not a stretch, it is a drowning.</strong> You gave the high-potential the hardest sprint in the firm's history with no mentor, no escalation and no cover for their day job. When they freeze at day 60 you will conclude they “weren't ready” — when actually you set them up to fail.`,
                    coach_r3_stretch_thin:  `<strong>The right person, a thin net.</strong> You stretched the high-potential — good instinct — but the scaffold is light. A bit more mentoring, a clearer escalation path and real day-job cover would turn a risky bet into a developmental one.`,
                    coach_r3_deputy:        `<strong>Safe — and you stretched no one.</strong> The deputy will likely deliver, but you just told your best young engineer this place will not bet on them, and you built no future leader. Laljani's research says leaders are made in the deep end. If you never stretch anyone, where does Pennine's next MD come from?`,
                    coach_r3_micromanage:   `<strong>A title with no authority is not a stretch.</strong> You appointed the high-potential then kept every real decision yourself. They cannot grow into a role you will not let them actually do. Give real authority — with guardrails, not a leash.`,
                    coach_r3_abandon:       `<strong>Free rein is not support.</strong> Handing over the hardest sprint in the firm's history and stepping away entirely is abandonment, not empowerment. A stretch needs a net — authority with guardrails and a mentor, not a vanishing act.`,
                    coach_r3_nofallback:    `<strong>No checkpoint means you fly blind.</strong> Without an agreed halfway review you will not see your stretch leader struggling until it is too late to add support without taking over. Plan the day-45 signals now.`,
                    coach_r3_swap:          `<strong>A swap-the-second-they-wobble plan is a self-fulfilling prophecy.</strong> If everyone knows the deputy is waiting to take over at the first wobble, the stretch leader leads scared and the team never commits. A fallback should add support, not stand over them with a hook.`,
                    coach_r4_focused:       `<strong>Focused recovery is the disciplined move.</strong> Pulling the external expertise and the rig onto the slipping subsystem while protecting the rest of scope recovers the unit within the cap. That is managerial delivery under pressure done right.`,
                    coach_r4_descope:       `<strong>Quietly dropping the subsystem is a credibility landmine.</strong> The customer issued a cure notice — they will qualify the unit thoroughly. When the missing subsystem surfaces, you have not saved the contract, you have detonated what trust was left.`,
                    coach_r4_overspend:     `<strong>You blew past the £3.5M cap and triggered the capital review.</strong> Throwing money at the problem may recover the subsystem, but you have lost division-level authority and signalled to the group that you cannot deliver inside your mandate. Confidence falls even if the unit lands.`,
                    coach_r4_coach:         `<strong>You coached, you did not rescue.</strong> You held the bar, asked the right questions and added support while letting them lead the recovery. They will come out of this a leader who has survived a setback — exactly how strategic leaders are forged.`,
                    coach_r4_rescue:        `<strong>You grabbed the wheel.</strong> You saved the subsystem and un-built the leader. They have now learned that leadership gets taken away under pressure. Coaching through a setback builds a leader; rescuing them un-builds one. How could you have held the bar without taking over?`,
                    coach_r4_abandon:       `<strong>“They weren't ready” became a self-fulfilling prophecy.</strong> You pulled the high-potential at the first real setback and confirmed every fear that this place will not back its people. You did not test their readiness — you manufactured their failure.`,
                    coach_r4_counteroffer:  `<strong>A blanket cash counter-offer buys time, not loyalty.</strong> Pay alone retains cynically and teaches people to threaten to leave to get a raise. The two remaining flight risks need role, growth and a reason to believe in the transition.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the flight risks costs you the future.</strong> Lose the remaining senior engineers and you crystallise the £0.9M replacement exposure and gut the capability you just spent the budget rebuilding. The unit is not the only thing worth keeping.`,
                    coach_r4_board_honest:  `<strong>That is a board-ready, honest read.</strong> Unit on track, the subsystem recovered, a developed leader, and a clear line from this delivery to the clean-motion direction — bold AND true. That is how a new MD turns 90 days into a mandate.`,
                    coach_r4_overclaim:     `<strong>Over-claiming to buy peace burns the one asset you can't refund.</strong> Promise total victory and the full roadmap next quarter too, and when reality lands short the board stops believing anything you say. Credible beats impressive.`,
                    coach_r4_gantt:         `<strong>A Gantt chart is not a strategy.</strong> You showed the unit is progressing and left direction out — so the board sees a competent project manager, not the strategic leader they appointed. You may win the contract and still lose the mandate.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
