                    insight_strategic:      `Strategic leader: you held visionary intent AND managerial delivery together, stretched a leader and built confidence on credible commitments. You qualified the unit, kept the £34M contract alive, and gave the board a direction worth backing — exactly Laljani's both-dimensions test.`,
                    insight_manager:        `Manager, not leader: you ran a tight sprint and may have hit the unit, but strategy clarity stayed low. When the board asks where the division is going, the answer is a Gantt chart. You managed; they appointed you to lead.`,
                    insight_speech:         `Inspiring speech, no unit: your direction was bold but execution fell short of the 90-day qualified unit. Visionary without managerial is a speech — and once the £34M contract walks, the vision is worth little.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed result: you neither fully balanced the visionary–managerial demands nor decisively failed. Re-examine where the crisis pulled you off-balance — usually toward the dimension you were already strong in.`,
                    insight_notalent:       `Talent readiness stayed low: you either never stretched anyone or stretched without a scaffold. Either way, Pennine ends the 90 days no closer to its next strategic leader — the developmental cost of playing safe or setting someone up to fail.`,
                    insight_madeleader:     `You made a leader: the high-potential led the hardest sprint in the firm's history and came out of a real setback stronger. That is the 70-20-10 engine of development — a leader forged in the deep end with support.`,
                    insight_lowconfidence:  `Stakeholder confidence stayed low — board and customer are not yet convinced of your mandate. Confidence is earned through credible commitments and visible early delivery, not promises; rebuild it before it gates your next move.`,
                    insight_capitalreview:  `You breached the £3.5M division authority and triggered a group capital review. Even if the unit landed, you signalled you cannot deliver inside your mandate — a costly way to win.`,
                    insight_contractlost:   `Confidence collapsed and the anchor customer walked: the £34M contract is gone and much of the £26M R&D investment is stranded. The transition bet is in serious doubt.`,

                    hint_capitalreview:     `You have breached the £3.5M cap and triggered a capital review. From here, every commitment must be credible and inside your authority to rebuild confidence.`,
                    hint_manager:           `Execution is strong but strategy clarity is low — you are managing, not leading. Set and defend a clear direction, or you will hit the unit and still lose the board's mandate.`,
                    hint_speech:            `Your direction is clear but execution is lagging the 90-day unit. A vision the customer never sees delivered is a speech — fund the capability gap and lock the sprint scope.`,
                    hint_talent:            `Talent readiness is weak. You are either not stretching anyone or stretching without support. Develop a leader by stretching them WITH a scaffold — that is how the next MD is made.`,
                    hint_confidence:        `Stakeholder confidence is dangerously low. Make commitments that are bold AND true, and show visible early delivery — credibility is the asset that buys strategy its time.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong on all four KPIs — visionary AND managerial, with a developed leader and real confidence. Hold the line: link the day-90 delivery to the long-term direction in your board read.`,

